A single bone can tell you whether an animal walked on two legs or four. Teeth show what it ate: blade-like teeth for meat, leaf-shaped teeth for plants. Rough patches on bones show where muscles attached, and growth rings inside show how old the animal was.

Some fossils keep skin prints, showing scales like a stamp in mud. A few duck-billed dinosaurs have been found with large areas of skin preserved, sometimes called dinosaur [[@mummies|mummies]].

{{story:archaeology}}

Earth's surface is restless. Plates move, mountains wear down, and rock sinks back into the hot interior to be melted and remade. So Earth's very first rocks are long gone. The oldest we have, from northern Canada, are about 4 billion years old.

Tiny crystals called zircons are tougher. A few from the Jack Hills in Western Australia are about 4.4 billion years old, which means solid ground and maybe even oceans existed very early. For the full age, scientists turn to [[@meteorites|meteorites]], leftover pieces from the time the planets formed. The oldest are about 4.57 billion years old. Rocks brought back from the Moon tell a similar story, and help explain [[@moon-formed|how the Moon formed]].

{{story:meteorites}}

Before any astronaut flies, ISRO plans uncrewed test flights. One is meant to carry Vyommitra, a half-humanoid [[@robots|robot]] with a head, arms and a body but no legs. Vyommitra can speak, watch the capsule's control panels and report back on temperature, air and other conditions, just as a crew member would.

{{story:robots}}

The Soviet Union and the United States sent [[@space-animals|dogs and chimpanzees]] into space before people. Today, robots and sensors can do that job instead.

Ordinary muscles can't be switched on and off by nerves 700 times a second. So how do mosquitoes manage it? Many insects, including flies, [[@pollinators|bees]] and beetles, have special flight [[@muscles|muscles]] that do not pull on the wings directly. Instead they squeeze the box-shaped middle of the body, called the thorax.

The thorax is springy. When one set of muscles squeezes it one way, it snaps back, which stretches the other set and triggers it to contract. The wings click up and down with each snap. One signal from the [[@nervous-system|nervous system]] can lead to many wingbeats. Dragonflies are different. Their muscles pull on each of their four wings directly, so each wing can move on its own. This lets them hover, dart sideways and even fly backwards.

{{story:bees-dance}}

In July 2012, a team from Northwest Normal University in China was studying ordinary [[@lightning|lightning]] on the Qinghai Plateau. They had high-speed cameras and spectrometers pointed at a storm. A spectrometer splits light into colours, a bit like a prism, showing which elements are glowing.

A bolt struck the ground. Then a glowing ball rose from the spot and moved for about a second and a half. Its light carried the fingerprints of silicon, iron and calcium, which are common in soil. The scientists published their results in 2014.

{{story:lightning}}

In 1874 Monet and his friends, including Pierre-Auguste Renoir, Camille Pissarro and Berthe Morisot, held their own exhibition in Paris. The official art world had often rejected their work.

One of Monet's paintings showed the harbour at Le Havre at dawn, with an orange sun over hazy blue water. He called it *Impression, Sunrise*. A critic named Louis Leroy wrote a mocking review and called the painters "Impressionists". They kept the name, and [[@impressionism|Impressionism]] became one of the best-known movements in art.

{{story:paris}}

In 1997 and 1998, a financial crisis spread across Asia, sinking currencies and businesses from [[@thailand|Thailand]] to [[@seoul|South Korea]]. Rich countries realised that big emerging economies, like India, China and Brazil, needed to be part of the conversation.

So in 1999, finance ministers and central bank heads from 19 countries and the EU began meeting as the G20. For years these meetings were quiet and technical. Then came the global financial crisis of 2008, the worst since the [[@great-depression|Great Depression]]. In November 2008, the US invited G20 leaders to Washington, D.C., for the first summit of presidents and prime ministers.

{{story:great-depression}}

The draft's first article began, "All men are born free and equal." Hansa Mehta, India's delegate and a fighter for women's education, objected. In many countries, she pointed out, "men" could be used to mean only men.

The commission changed it to "all human beings". That small edit is still in the declaration, and the UN now credits her for it. On 10 December 1948, in Paris, the UN General Assembly voted to adopt the declaration. Nobody voted against it, though eight countries chose not to vote.

{{story:united-nations}}

After the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]], the [[@united-nations|United Nations]] wrote the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, adopted on 10 December 1948. Hansa Mehta, an Indian delegate, is credited with changing a line about "all men" to "all human beings", so it clearly included women.

{{story:united-nations}}

Countries do not always keep these promises. But the declaration gave people everywhere a shared language for saying when they were being treated unfairly, and it is still quoted in courts and protests around the world.

Each letter has a number code. Capital A is 65, which in binary is 01000001. Small a is 97. Every message on your phone is stored this way, one byte (eight bits) at a time for simple English letters. Hindi, Tamil and emoji need longer codes, but the idea is the same.

Pictures work like this too. A screen is a grid of tiny dots called [[@pixels|pixels]], and each one stores how much red, green and blue to show, all as binary numbers. A three-minute song saved on a phone can take about 3 million bytes. That is roughly 24 million switches set just so.

{{story:pixels}}

The most famous puzzle in game theory goes like this. Two suspects are questioned in separate rooms. If both stay silent, both get a short sentence. If one blames the other, the blamer goes free and the other gets a long sentence. If both blame each other, both get a medium sentence.

Each prisoner thinks: whatever the other does, I'm better off blaming them. So both blame, and both end up worse off than if they had trusted each other. The puzzle shows why cooperation can be hard even when it helps everyone, from countries cutting pollution to teammates sharing work.

{{story:cold-war}}

India has many religions and regions, and each has its own wedding customs. A Muslim wedding centres on the nikah, a contract agreed in front of witnesses. Christian couples in Kerala or Goa may marry in a church, then hold a big lunch.

Many North Indian weddings follow a pattern. At the [[@mehendi|mehendi]], women's hands are painted with henna. At the haldi, [[@turmeric|turmeric]] paste is rubbed on the couple's skin. The sangeet is a night of music and dance, often to [[@film-songs|film songs]]. Then comes the baraat, where the groom arrives on a horse or in a car, with relatives dancing in the street to a brass band.

{{story:indian-calendars}}

To drive a black cab in London, drivers must pass a test called "The Knowledge". They learn about 25,000 streets and thousands of landmarks, and it often takes three or four years. They may not use a map or GPS during the test.

In 2000, scientists in London scanned taxi drivers' brains. The back part of their hippocampus was larger than in other people, and the longer they had driven, the bigger it tended to be. Later studies followed trainees and found the change appeared in those who passed. It showed that learning a city can actually reshape the brain.

{{story:pigeons-homing}}

Your skin is a whole world of different neighbourhoods. Oily places like your forehead host different bacteria from dry ones like your forearm, and damp ones like your armpits. Body odour mostly comes from bacteria breaking down sweat.

In 2012, scientists at North Carolina State University swabbed the belly buttons of 60 volunteers. They found more than 2,300 kinds of bacteria, and some were possibly new to science. A few kinds appeared in most people, but every belly button had its own mix. Your [[@gross-belly-button|belly button]] may be one of the least-washed places on your body.

{{story:microbes-friendly}}

Some of the hardest stretches crossed the Taklamakan Desert and the high Pamir mountains. Traders travelled in [[caravan|caravans]] for safety, often with two-humped Bactrian [[@camels|camels]] that can cope with freezing nights and blazing days.

Along the way grew oasis towns such as Dunhuang, Kashgar and Samarkand. They had markets, inns called caravanserais, temples and wells. These towns became rich by serving travellers, and their people spoke many languages. Samarkand, in today's Uzbekistan, sat at a crossroads where routes from China, India and Persia met, and it grew into one of the great cities of Central Asia.

{{story:camels}}

The Nile helped build [[@ancient-egypt|ancient Egypt]]. Boats sailed north with the current and south with the wind, which usually blows from the north. Huge stone blocks for temples and [[@pyramids|pyramids]] were floated on barges. Papyrus reeds growing along the banks were made into paper and boats.

{{story:pyramids}}

The flood even shaped their [[@calendars|calendar]]. Egyptians noticed that the bright star Sirius reappeared before dawn at about the time the flood began. They divided the year into three seasons: flood, growing and harvest.

Scholars offer several reasons. Animals come with ready-made personalities. Most listeners already think of a jackal as cunning and a lion as proud, which saves the storyteller time. Animals also make stories safe. A minister could tell a king a story about a stupid lion without being thrown into prison.

Animal stories are memorable and funny too, which matters for children and for princes who would rather not study. In a world where most people lived close to forests and fields, the animals were familiar neighbours. The fables of [[@aesop|Aesop]], from [[@ancient-greece|ancient Greece]], use the same trick, and so do India's own [[@jataka-tales|Jataka tales]].

{{story:croc-ancient}}

In 1957, the cartoonist K. Shankar Pillai set up the Children's Book Trust in Delhi. He wanted good, cheap books written for Indian children, not just copies of British ones. The Trust also ran writing competitions that found new authors.

Ten years later, in 1967, Anant Pai began [[@amar-chitra-katha|Amar Chitra Katha]], comics retelling myths, legends and history. In 1980 he started *Tinkle*, whose characters like Suppandi made a whole generation laugh.

{{story:printing-press}}

Ancient Greek poets, writing about 2,500 years ago, mentioned *tagenites*, cakes of wheat flour cooked in olive oil and eaten with honey. The Romans had something similar. In south India, dosa is mentioned in Tamil writings from more than a thousand years ago.

Pancakes probably go back much further than any written record, because they need so little. Ground grain, water and a flat stone heated beside a fire are enough. A hot stone works perfectly well as a tawa.

{{story:ancient-greece}}

Some Indian pickles are fermented, which means friendly microbes are allowed to grow on purpose. In Punjab, a winter pickle called kanji is a purple drink made by fermenting black carrots in water with mustard seeds.

In Northeast India, [[@northeast-food|bamboo shoot pickles]] are fermented for a sour, tangy taste. These [[@microbes-friendly|friendly microbes]] are related to the ones that turn milk into curd.

{{story:microbes-friendly}}
